//! Differential testing of the zero-dimensional pipeline against an external computer algebra
//! system.
//!
//! M17 validated the conversion against 29 Singular reference bases, committed as literals and
//! regenerated by hand. That established the conventions but left the oracle out of the
//! development loop: it could not be pointed at a thousand random systems, and it compared only
//! the final lex basis — so a wrong multiplication-operator column could only be found if it
//! happened to change the answer.
//!
//! This module makes the oracle a tool. It generates deterministic systems, asks Singular about
//! each one, and compares **every intermediate result**: the source Gröbner basis, the quotient
//! dimension, the standard monomials, the normal form of each border monomial, each
//! multiplication-operator column, and the lex basis. Each of those has a precise mathematical
//! answer, which is what makes it a unit worth comparing rather than an internal detail.
//!
//! ```text
//! cargo run --release --bin zeebra-differential -- --systems 500
//! ```
//!
//! # CI does not need Singular
//! A sweep's agreeing cases can be emitted as [`DifferentialFixture`] literals and committed. An
//! ordinary Rust test then replays the same comparison against the recorded answers, so the checks
//! CI runs are the checks the development machine runs — with a table of constants where the
//! subprocess was. See [`fixture`].
//!
//! # An oracle that did not answer is not agreement
//! Singular exits 0 after a script error. A timeout, a missing binary, a characteristic outside
//! Singular's range, or an answer Singular contradicts itself on each produce an
//! [`OracleError`], which [`SweepReport::clean`](sweep::SweepReport::clean)
//! counts as a failure of the sweep.

pub mod check;
pub mod comparison;
pub mod field;
pub mod fixture;
pub mod generator;
pub mod singular;
pub mod sweep;
pub mod text;

/// Oracle answers recorded from Singular. Test-only: it is reference data, not library code.
#[cfg(test)]
mod fixtures;

/// The GF(2^8) half of the same, recorded by its own sweep.
///
/// A second table rather than more rows in the first: a generated header's regeneration command is
/// only true of the sweep that wrote it, and adding a seventh field to the first sweep's list would
/// change which field every seed above index 23 is generated over — so the 240 recorded prime
/// answers would all be replaced to add one GF(2^8) row.
#[cfg(test)]
mod fixtures_gf256;

/// The Fukuoka MQ Challenge's GF(2^8), recorded by its own sweep.
///
/// A third table for the reason there is a second: a generated header's regeneration command is
/// only true of the sweep that wrote it. It is a *different field* from `fixtures_gf256`'s, not a
/// second sample of it — `0x57 * 0x83` is `0xc1` modulo `0x11B` and `0x52` modulo `0x11D` — so the
/// answers here are not derivable from those there (#421).
#[cfg(test)]
mod fixtures_gf256mq;

